<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Masaesyli</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 202 BCE (approx.) · capital Siga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Berber kingdom of western Numidia under Syphax, a power courted by both Rome and Carthage in the Second Punic War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Masaesyli were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> people of western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. By the late 3rd century BCE they formed a kingdom ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siga">Siga</a>, near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafna">Tafna</a> river, with a second capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> conquered eastern lands. Their rise came amid the struggle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> ruled a large realm of pastoral and farming tribes, minted coins with his portrait in Hellenistic style and kept a strong cavalry. He first made contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and received envoys from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a>, but in 205 BCE married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophonisba">Sophonisba</a>, daughter of the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco">Hasdrubal</a>, and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. A royal mausoleum at Beni Rhenane near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siga">Siga</a> is attributed to his dynasty.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 203 BCE Roman and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massylii">Massylii</a> forces defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> and captured him. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vermina">Vermina</a> ruled a reduced western kingdom for a time, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Zama</a> in 202 BCE most Masaesylian land fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa's</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/masaesyli.jpg" alt="Masaesyli" width="320" height="185"><figcaption>Image: User:Redtony, User:Ras67, User:Amble · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numidia_220_BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 202 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Siga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Syphax, Vermina</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Berber religion, Punic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Numidian (Libyco-Berber), Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of the Masaesyli, Western Numidia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-251&amp;focus=masaesyli" aria-label="Masaesyli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4643 903 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Masaesyli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4963 1159L5166 1122"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5166 1122h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4963 1159h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4963 1159h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Siga. <a href="/atlas?year=-251&amp;focus=masaesyli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

